  CTX-0060 [convention] MAY_BE_STALE (CHANGELOG.md changed) — Release process (from 1.0.0): - The package follows SemVer; each release is one commit with an annotated tag vX.Y.Z matching package.json and src/version.js; tags are never moved. - Before release: npm ci + npm run check, doctor on the self-dogfood workspace, npm audit with every finding classified, npm pack --dry-run plus inspection and secret scan of the real tarball, a packed-candidate smoke test from an isolated prefix, provider/network scan, git diff --check. - CHANGELOG.md and docs/upgrading-to-*.md are updated per release; GitHub release notes live in .github/release-notes/. - Publishing: GitHub Release -> release.yml -> npm Trusted Publishing with provenance; the trusted publisher is created when the first automated release is ready because npm expires an unused configuration after 2 days. - package.json files ships src, resources, docs, README.md, CHANGELOG.md and LICENSE. - Nothing is committed, tagged, pushed or published without the maintainer's explicit approval.
  CTX-0069 [convention] MAY_BE_STALE (docs/connected-requirements-and-quality.md, docs/limitations.md, docs/upgrading-to-v1.1.0.md changed) — Documentation set: - README.md is the simple first-use journey; docs/using-yallaflow.md is the practical developer guide. - docs/cli.md is the command reference. - docs/architecture.md, docs/project-memory.md and docs/shared-projects.md cover the design; docs/connected-requirements-and-quality.md is the guide to ticket import, AI QC and Smart Demo. - docs/security.md and docs/limitations.md cover trust boundaries and gaps; docs/releasing.md covers the release policy. - docs/upgrading-to-*.md are per-version upgrade notes; .github/release-notes/ holds GitHub release notes.
  CTX-0073 [architecture] MAY_BE_STALE (test/quality-qc.test.js changed) — Verification evidence: 'yallaflow verify' runs argv commands by default (spawnSync with shell:false); --shell and --script are explicit alternatives. Each run is appended to work/<id>/evidence/verification.json with its full stdout/stderr log file, capturing up to 64 MiB of output (v1.1.0; Node's 1 MiB default had recorded verbose suites as failed). Recognizable secret values are redacted in the log and in the recorded command; the executed command is unchanged. Failed runs are kept. Optional QC and demo records never write this ledger.
  CTX-0080 [architecture] MAY_BE_STALE (src/quality/report.js changed) — Optional Smart Demo (v1.1.0, src/quality/demo.js) is independent of QC: on explicit request the Agent proposes prioritized user-facing scenarios (demo propose: audience business|developer, priority, criteria, rationale, steps, record flag, excluded items), the developer's explicit approval is recorded with their words and bound to that exact proposal's SHA-256 (demo approve --note [--only]), and demo record is refused for an unapproved or changed proposal or scenarios outside the approved scope. Approved scenarios may be split across several recordings (one video per feature or audience); coverage of the approved scope is computed across all runs of the approved proposal (approvedCoverage), never per video. Video is optional (Playwright video-start/chapter/stop; needs Playwright's ffmpeg) and stored hashed under work/<id>/quality/media/; every run carries a fixed 'not test evidence' disclaimer, and convergence refuses demo: citations. Demo and QC share the local artifact store and the report.md/report.html generator.
  CTX-0082 [architecture] MAY_BE_STALE (src/quality/artifacts.js, docs/security.md changed) — Security boundaries: - Repository evidence must be a relative path inside the root. - Source writes are confined to their directory, and quality artifacts to a folder created exclusively per record, work/<id>/quality/media/<record-id>-<random>/; integrity checks read media only when its real path is inside that work item's media folder (symlinks pointing elsewhere are reported, never followed). - verify is not a sandbox. - Write authorization, approvals, demo approvals, strict QC quality approval and Shared Project roles are workflow guidance, not access control. - There is no secrets store: tracker credentials for intake ticket --fetch are read from environment variables per request, sent only over https to the hosted tracker (api.github.com, *.atlassian.net, dev.azure.com, *.visualstudio.com) or to an origin the project configured explicitly — never to a host taken only from a ticket URL, and never persisted; doctor rejects credential-like keys in requirements.providers config. - One pattern-based sanitizer (src/security/redact.js) redacts recognizable secret values at YallaFlow's persistence boundary: structured state, the ledger and its projection, work records, ADRs, logs, source text representations, and quality ledgers and reports. It never modifies developer files, preserved source originals, or copied quality media.
